  "reason": "The records collectively make the later study plausibly reconstructible. R3 directly establishes orientation-dependent uniaxial tension and dislocation-mediated deformation in the closely related FCC FeCoNiCrMn alloy. R1 supplies the molecular-dynamics design of varying temperature and high strain rate during HEA tension, while R4 establishes stacking-fault-bearing deformation bands in the Cantor alloy and R10 directly connects dislocations, stacking faults, and stacking-fault energetics in FCC Fe-Ni molecular dynamics. Other records reinforce thermal softening, rate-dependent dislocation density, dislocation slip, and suppression or absence of twinning. The precise claim that vacancy clusters initiate yielding, as well as the exact orientation ranking and strain-rate-insensitive yield strength, is not directly disclosed, but an expert could plausibly combine the earlier results to propose the later intervention and its partial-dislocation/stacking-fault mechanism."
